    </section>

    <section id="boundaries" class="study-section tinted" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 / Boundaries first</p>
        <h2 id="boundaries-title">Three services, each owning its API, queue, workers, and database.</h2>
        <p>
          Scale-out starts with ownership. A service can only scale on its own if nothing else
          reads its database or its queue. Services talk only through APIs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="figure-1">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{architectureDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Figure 1</span> High-level architecture. Numbered edges are the client's calls, in order.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="arch" aria-label="Load shape of each service and the external dependencies">
        <ol class="arch-services">
          {services.map((service, index) => (
            <li>
              <p class="mono-label">Step {index + 1}</p>
              <h3>{service.name}</h3>
              <p>{service.role}</p>
              <ul>{service.owns.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
              <p class="arch-load">{service.load}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
        <div class="arch-external">
          <p class="mono-label">External, not services</p>
          <ul>
            {externals.map((item) => (
              <li><strong>{item.name}</strong><span>{item.note}</span></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The client ingests a dataset, waits for <code>READY</code>, then submits the signed
          dataset reference that ingestion issued, with a grader UUID and model config, to
          ExecutionService. Execution verifies the signature and tenant, and never accepts a raw
          blob URI. Execution events
          reach AggregationService through a retrying outbox. The client reads the report there.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The hardest calls were about what is <em>not</em> a service. The LLM API is an external
          provider. Blob Storage is managed infrastructure, not a fourth microservice. The grader
          is a library that runs inside the execution worker, so grading costs CPU, not a network
          round trip. Keeping these out of the service count kept the design small and made the
          real bottleneck obvious.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ingestion also never sees the model config or the grader UUID. A dataset is reusable
          across many models and graders, so those are execution concerns. The client chains the
          two calls itself, as <a href="#figure-2">Figure 2</a> shows end to end.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram" id="figure-2">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{endToEndDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Figure 2</span> End-to-end flow, from dataset URL to final report.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="scale-out" class="study-section" aria-labelledby="scale-out-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">05 / Scaling out</p>
        <h2 id="scale-out-title">Find the real bottleneck, then scale against it.</h2>
        <p>
          The three services have very different load shapes. That is the main reason to keep
          them separate: each one scales on its own signal.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="reasoning-chain" aria-label="Scale-out reasoning">
        <li>
          <span>Partition</span>
          <p>The unit of work is one question. Task identity is <code>(execution_id, record_id)</code>, so any worker can pick up any task.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Bypass</span>
          <p>Execution workers read dataset chunks straight from Blob. Large bytes never pass through ingestion, so ingestion stays off the hot path.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Bottleneck</span>
          <p>Execution is bound by the LLM provider and grader CPU, not by worker count. More workers past that point only create 429s.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Limit</span>
          <p>Admit every model call through a limiter keyed on the provider's quota: requests per minute, tokens per minute, and calls in flight, with a fair share per tenant. Respect the provider's <code>Retry-After</code>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Signal</span>
          <p>Autoscale on queue depth, oldest message age, end-to-end latency, CPU, memory, and remaining provider budget together.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="scale-plane">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{scalePlaneDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Scale-out data plane</span> <span>Workers are stateless competing consumers. The quota limiter, not the worker count, sets the rate at the provider.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="math-band">
        <article>
          <p class="mono-label">A queue is not capacity</p>
          <h3>1,000 in, 200 out, per minute.</h3>
          <p>
            Backlog grows by 800 tasks every minute. After five minutes, about 4,000 tasks are
            waiting, and it never recovers. A queue absorbs bursts. It does not fix sustained
            overload. Only admission control or more <em>safe</em> capacity does.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="mono-label">Little's Law</p>
          <h3 set:html={tex(String.raw`L = \lambda W`)} />
          <p>
            At 100 tasks per second and 5 seconds in the system, about 500 tasks are in flight.
            That sizes the concurrency I need to hold. It says nothing about what the provider
            will actually allow, so I check both numbers.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>Admission control</h3>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            <li>Cap outstanding jobs globally, per tenant, and per evaluation, by count and by estimated cost.</li>
            <li>Return <code>429</code> for a tenant quota. Return <code>503</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> for service-wide overload.</li>
            <li>Never return <code>202</code> until the job and its enqueue intent are durable.</li>
            <li>Use fair scheduling so one large evaluation cannot starve small tenants.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Queue choice</h3>
          <p>
            This is task distribution, not stream processing. A Service Bus or SQS style queue
            gives me leases, delayed retries, dead-letter queues, and competing consumers out of
            the box. Kafka earns its cost when ordered replay and high-throughput streams are core
            requirements. Here they are not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="temporal" class="study-section" aria-labelledby="temporal-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 / Temporal mini workers</p>
        <h2 id="temporal-title">Temporal runs the task lifecycle on small, single-purpose workers.</h2>
        <p>
          The scaled design needs durable per-question state, retries with backoff, liveness
          checks, a provider rate limit, and cancellation. Built by hand, that is a state table,
          retry-wait states, lease renewal, an outbox publisher, and a distributed limiter.
          Temporal provides each of these as a primitive, so ExecutionService's workers become
          Temporal mini workers: small pools that each do one kind of work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Temporal is infrastructure inside ExecutionService, not a fourth service. Only
          ExecutionService talks to it, so the three-service boundary holds.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="temporal-pools">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{temporalPoolsDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Mini worker pools</span> <span>One task queue per kind of work. The generate queue carries the provider's rate limit.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="evolution-title">Why Temporal, reason by reason</h3>
      <ol class="reason-grid">
        {temporalReasons.map((reason, index) => (
          <li>
            <span class="mono-label">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h4>{reason.title}</h4>
            <p set:html={mathText(reason.body)} />
            <p class="replaces"><strong>Replaces:</strong> {reason.replaces}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="temporal-flow">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{temporalFlowDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Workflow shape</span> <span>A parent workflow per evaluation, a child per batch, and three activities per question.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>Costs and limits</h3>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            {temporalLimits.map((limit) => <li>{limit}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Two sources of truth, one each</h3>
          <p>
            Temporal owns orchestration: which step each question is on, timers, and retries. The
            Results DB owns the domain: answers, attempts, verdicts, and the outbox. Every
            activity commits to the DB before it returns, and the history carries only IDs. If
            the two disagree after a crash, the DB wins, because each activity reads it before
            acting.
          </p>
          <h3>The quota limiter</h3>
          <p>
            Each generate activity asks a limiter inside ExecutionService for a slot before it
            calls the model. The limiter is keyed on the provider's quota (account or project,
            plus model or deployment) and holds three budgets: requests per minute, tokens per
            minute (charged with the prompt size plus <code>max_tokens</code>, refunded to actual
            usage after the call), and calls in flight. Within a quota, tenants get weighted fair
            shares.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="evolution-title">Retry semantics, failure by failure</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="retry-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Failure point</th><th scope="col">What happens</th><th scope="col">Cost</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {retrySemantics.map((row) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{row.when}</th><td>{row.what}</td><td>{row.cost}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="claims" class="study-section tinted" aria-labelledby="claims-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">07 / Scalability claims</p>
        <h2 id="claims-title">What scales, how far, and what stops it.</h2>
        <p>
          These are design claims, derived from the mechanisms above. Each one names the bound
          that limits it and the condition that breaks it. Section 08 sizes the system against an
          estimated workload.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="claims">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Claim</th><th scope="col">Mechanism</th><th scope="col">Bound</th><th scope="col">Breaks when</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {claims.map((row) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{row.claim}</th><td>{row.mechanism}</td><td set:html={mathText(row.bound)} /><td set:html={mathText(row.breaks)} /></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <aside class="sizing" aria-label="Planning example">
        <p class="mono-label">Planning example: a small provider quota</p>
        <h3>The ceiling is the provider, not the worker count.</h3>
        <p>
          Suppose the provider allows 100 calls in flight and a call takes 5 seconds. That is
          <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`100 / 5 = 20`)} /> calls per second, or
          <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`20 \times 3600 / 500 = 144`)} /> evaluations of 500
          questions per hour, however many workers are added. A queue of 10,000 such evaluations
          is <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\tfrac{10\,000 \times 500}{20} = 250\,000`)} />
          seconds, about 69 hours, before retries. Within that budget the limiter gives each
          tenant a weighted fair share. When the provider returns 429s, the limiter honors
          <code>Retry-After</code> and halves the in-flight cap, so retries slow new work instead
          of piling on top of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 1,000-per-hour sizing target (section 08) therefore needs a much larger quota:
          about 8,000 calls in flight.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section id="results" class="study-section" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">08 / Sizing estimate</p>
        <h2 id="results-title">Sized for 1,000 evaluations per hour.</h2>
        <p>
          These figures are estimates from environment math, not measurements from a run. The
          sizing target is 1,000 evaluations per hour, where one evaluation is a full run of about
          500 questions, with an estimated latency of 24 minutes at P50, 26 minutes at P95, and
          60 minutes at P99. The provider, model, and quota are not published, so the table below
          derives what any deployment needs to reach that target.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="status-split" aria-label="What exists today and what is estimated or proposed">
        <div>
          <p class="mono-label">Exists today</p>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            <li>A model-agnostic, multi-turn evaluation harness: one interface for RL, fine-tuned, and hosted models.</li>
            <li>Per-turn OpenTelemetry spans for the session, each model turn, and each tool call.</li>
            <li>Full artifact capture on every turn, viewable next to its trace in Jaeger.</li>
            <li>Model attempts logged separately from conversation results.</li>
            <li>Re-grading saved outputs without re-running the agent. The grader is itself a model, so re-grading still makes model calls.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="mono-label">Estimated or proposed in this study</p>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            <li>The throughput and latency figures on this page: estimates from environment math.</li>
            <li>Temporal mini workers and the quota limiter.</li>
            <li>Parent-run lineage for retries.</li>
            <li>Prometheus metrics and Grafana dashboards.</li>
            <li>Re-grading with zero model calls, which needs a deterministic grader.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="workload-table">
          <caption>Workload model. Estimates come from environment math, not a measured run; the rest is derived from them or is a stated design default.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {workloadRows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.q}</th>
                {row.math ? <td set:html={row.v} /> : <td>{row.v}</td>}
                <td><span class={`src src-${row.src.split(/[ ,(]/)[0].toLowerCase()}`}>{row.src}</span></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="latency">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{latencyChart}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Estimated latency</span> <span>Estimated end-to-end time per evaluation at P50, P95, and P99. Not measured.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The derived numbers are the point. Sustaining 1,000 evaluations per hour would take about
          139 questions per second, about 400 evaluations and 8,000 model calls in flight, and
          about 8,300 requests per minute at the provider. At 20 calls per evaluation, a 24-minute
          run also implies close to a minute per model call, which fits long reasoning outputs,
          not 5-second completions. A quota smaller than that caps throughput below 1,000 per
          hour, whatever the worker count.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the estimate, P50 and P95 sit two minutes apart and P99 is 2.3 times P95: a
          predictable body with a long tail. Whether a real deployment has that shape is for
          measurement to establish. Each run's trace splits its time into queue wait
          (schedule-to-start), limiter wait, generation, grading, and retry backoff. If the extra
          30-odd minutes at P99 sit in limiter wait and backoff, the tail is throttling; if they
          sit in queue wait, it is admission and backlog; if a few questions dominate, it is long
          generations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="issues" class="study-section tinted" aria-labelledby="issues-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">09 / Main issues at scale</p>
        <h2 id="issues-title">Six issues at this scale, and how the design answers each.</h2>
