    <section id="failure" class="study-section tinted" aria-labelledby="failure-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">10 / What breaks at scale</p>
        <h2 id="failure-title">More workers means more duplicates. Design for them.</h2>
        <p>
          With competing consumers, a message will be delivered twice and a worker will die
          mid-task. "Idempotent" is not one property here. It is five, and each one guards a
          different failure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Guards against</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {idempotency.map((row) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{row.layer}</th><td><code>{row.key}</code></td><td>{row.guards}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>Persist the answer before grading</h3>
          <p>
            Each task moves through the states in <a href="#figure-3">Figure 3</a>, with
            retry-wait states on both the generation and grading steps. The generated answer
            is stored before grading starts. If the grader fails, the retry resumes at grading
            against the stored answer. It never pays for a second LLM call, and it never grades a
            different, nondeterministic answer.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Outbox in, inbox out</h3>
          <p>
            The worker commits the task result and an outbox event in one transaction. A publisher
            calls AggregationService and retries with backoff until accepted. AggregationService
            inserts into an inbox keyed by <code>event_id</code>, so a duplicate is acknowledged
            and dropped. That is at-least-once delivery with idempotent processing
            (<a href="#figure-4">Figure 4</a>).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram" id="figure-3">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{lifecycleDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Figure 3</span> Worker task lifecycle. A grading retry never goes back through generation.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="figure-4">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{outboxDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Figure 4</span> Transactional outbox in ExecutionService, deduplicating inbox in AggregationService.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <aside class="definition-band" aria-label="Exactly-once caveat">
        <p class="mono-label">The caveat I state up front</p>
        <p>
          I do not promise exactly-once LLM calls. The provider may finish a request after my
          worker times out. I record every attempt, use provider idempotency keys where they
          exist, and make commits idempotent. The goal is effectively-once business state, not
          exactly-once side effects.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section id="metrics" class="study-section" aria-labelledby="metrics-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">11 / Honest metrics</p>
        <h2 id="metrics-title">Count from task events, not from worker counters.</h2>
        <p>
          AggregationService derives counts from deduplicated terminal task events. A worker's
          cumulative counter can be repeated or arrive out of order. A task event cannot be
          counted twice.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="formula-grid">
        <div><dt>accuracy</dt><dd set:html={tex(String.raw`\frac{\text{correct}}{\text{graded}}`, true)} /></div>
        <div><dt>coverage</dt><dd set:html={tex(String.raw`\frac{\text{graded}}{\text{total}}`, true)} /></div>
        <div><dt>terminal invariant</dt><dd set:html={tex(String.raw`\text{graded} + \text{failed} + \text{cancelled} = \text{total}`, true)} /></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A failed question is not a wrong answer. If it were counted as wrong, a provider
          outage would look like a model regression. Reporting coverage next to accuracy keeps
          the two visible. The run finalizes only when every task is terminal, or when the
          cancellation policy says it is.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also keep a reproducibility fingerprint over snapshot hash, model config and version,
          grader UUID and version, and execution config. It is <em>not</em> an idempotency key.
          Running the same config twice on purpose is a valid experiment. Only the API
          idempotency key identifies a retried request.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>


    <section id="decisions" class="study-section tinted" aria-labelledby="decisions-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">12 / Decision log</p>
        <h2 id="decisions-title">The trade-offs, in one place.</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="decision-grid">
        {decisions.map((item) => (
          <div><dt>{item.q}</dt><dd>{item.a}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <div class="staff-lesson">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I would carry to the next design</p>
        <h3>Scale-out is mostly a boundary and correctness problem, not a worker-count problem.</h3>
        <p>
          Adding workers is the easy part. The design work is deciding who owns which state,
          finding the dependency that actually limits throughput, and making every retry safe.
          Out of scope on purpose: a server-side orchestration service. The client can chain
          ingest and execute; I would add an orchestrator only if the problem required it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="validity" class="study-section" aria-labelledby="validity-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">13 / Advanced extension: measurement validity</p>
        <h2 id="validity-title">A score is a measurement: it needs an error bar and a validated grader.</h2>
        <p>
          Sections 01 to 12 are the core design. The next two extend it, and the platform runs
          without them. The core makes the numbers correct under failure. This extension makes
          them mean something. Every report carries a standard error, names the grader version it was
          measured under, and links to evidence of how well that grader agrees with humans. It
          adds no service: calibration sets are datasets, validation is a grade-only execution,
          and every statistic is an aggregation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="diagram fit" id="figure-5">
        <div class="diagram-scroll"><pre class="mermaid">{validationDiagram}</pre></div>
        <figcaption><span class="mono-label">Figure 5</span> <span>Grader validation and statistical reporting, inside the same three services.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <ul class="formula-cards">
        {validityFormulas.map((f) => (
          <li>
            <p class="mono-label">{f.name}</p>
            <div class="formula" set:html={f.html} />
            <p class="formula-note">{f.note}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Here <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\hat{Z}`)} /> is the grader's 0/1 verdict and
          <Fragment set:html={tex('Z')} /> the human one. Sensitivity
          <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`q_1 = P(\hat{Z} = 1 \mid Z = 1)`)} /> and specificity
          <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`q_0 = P(\hat{Z} = 0 \mid Z = 0)`)} /> come from a
          human-labeled calibration set. A grader version can be pinned only after it has a
          validation report, and the report is stratified by how much the humans agree with each
          other. The next section shows why that stratification is not optional.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="alignment" class="study-section tinted" aria-labelledby="alignment-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">14 / Advanced extension: judge alignment</p>
        <h2 id="alignment-title">Does an LLM judge agree with humans? Split the question first.</h2>
        <p>
          I tested the validation rules on public data: GPT-4 as a judge (G-Eval, 20 sampled
          scores per item) against the three SummEval expert annotators. That is
          {alignment.meta.items_per_dimension.toLocaleString('en-US')} summaries of
          {alignment.meta.documents} news articles from {alignment.meta.systems} systems, on four
          dimensions. Scores become 0/1 verdicts as on this platform: pass means 4 or 5 out of 5
          (3 out of 3 for fluency).
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="alignment-defs">
        <div><dt>Calibration</dt><dd>Does the judge pass items as often as humans do? Pass rate, sensitivity, specificity.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Discrimination</dt><dd>Does it rank clearly good items above clearly bad ones? AUC on items all experts agree on.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Rater equivalence</dt><dd>Could it replace one expert? Judge-expert vs expert-expert agreement, per agreement stratum.</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <figure class="agreement-figure" id="agreement-chart">
        <ul class="chart-legend" aria-label="Legend">
          <li><span class="swatch" style={`background:${SERIES.hh.color}`}></span>{SERIES.hh.label}</li>
          <li><span class="swatch" style={`background:${SERIES.hm.color}`}></span>{SERIES.hm.label}</li>
          <li><span class="swatch tick-swatch"></span>Random labeler at the judge's pass rate</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="agreement-scroll"><div class="agreement-panels">
          <div>
            <h3 class="panel-title">All three experts agree</h3>
            <Fragment set:html={unanimousPanel} />
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="panel-title">Experts split 2 to 1</h3>
            <Fragment set:html={splitPanel} />
          </div>
        </div></div>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="mono-label">Figure 6</span>
          <span>Share of verdicts that match one expert. On split items two experts agree 1/3 of the time by construction, so any labeler near the random line looks "better than human".</span>
        </figcaption>
        <details class="chart-data">
          <summary>Data table</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap tight">
            <table class="alignment-table">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">Items</th><th scope="col">n</th><th scope="col">Expert vs expert</th><th scope="col">Judge vs expert</th><th scope="col">Random labeler</th><th scope="col">Judge <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\kappa`)} /></th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {dims.flatMap((d) => (['unanimous', 'split'] as const).map((st) => (
                  <tr><th scope="row">{d.label}</th><td>{st === 'unanimous' ? 'Agree' : 'Split'}</td><td>{d.binary[st].n}</td><td>{fmt(d.binary[st].hh_agreement)}</td><td>{fmt(d.binary[st].hm_agreement)}</td><td>{fmt(d.binary[st].random_agreement)}</td><td>{fmt(d.binary[st].hm_kappa)}</td></tr>
                )))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      </figure>

      <ol class="finding-list">
        <li>
          <h3>Pooled numbers mix two regimes.</h3>
          <p>
            On split items the judge matches one expert {range(alignmentFacts.splitHM)} of the
            time, above expert-expert agreement of 0.33. A random labeler with the judge's pass
            rate scores {range(alignmentFacts.splitRandom)}, and the judge's <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\kappa`)} /> there is
            {range(alignmentFacts.splitKappa)}. The "advantage" is arithmetic, not skill. Split
            items are {range(alignmentFacts.splitShare, pct)} of each dimension, so a pooled
            agreement is not comparable across dimensions.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Calibration fails; discrimination mostly holds.</h3>
          <p>
            The judge passes {range(alignmentFacts.judgePass, pct)} of items where the experts pass
            {range(alignmentFacts.humanPass, pct)}. Yet on items all experts agree on, its AUC is
            {range(alignmentFacts.aucUnanimous)}. With the threshold refit on half the documents
            and tested on the other half, judge-expert <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\kappa`)} /> rises from {range(alignmentFacts.kappaRaw)}
            to {range(alignmentFacts.kappaRecal)}, against expert-expert <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\kappa`)} /> of {range(alignmentFacts.kappaHH)}.
            On split items the AUC is only {range(alignmentFacts.aucSplit)}.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Rankings survive where item verdicts do not.</h3>
          <p>
            Kendall's <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\tau`)} /> between judge and expert rankings of the 16 systems is
            {range(alignmentFacts.tau)}, while item-level <Fragment set:html={tex(String.raw`\kappa`)} /> is {range(alignmentFacts.kappaRaw)}. A
            judge can be fit to rank systems and unfit to grade single answers.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="alignment-table">
          <caption>Correcting the pass rate with sensitivity and specificity from a calibration half of the documents ({alignment.dimensions.coherence.correction_check.reps} random splits). Coverage is how often the 95% interval contains the true human pass rate.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">Error, raw</th><th scope="col">Error, corrected</th><th scope="col">Coverage, closed form</th><th scope="col">Coverage, cluster bootstrap</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {dims.map((d) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{d.label}</th><td>{fmt(d.correction_check.mean_abs_error_raw)}</td><td>{fmt(d.correction_check.mean_abs_error_corrected)}</td><td>{pct(d.correction_check.closed_form_ci95_coverage)}</td><td>{pct(d.correction_check.cluster_bootstrap_ci95_coverage)}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The correction works: error falls from {range(alignmentFacts.rawErr)} to
          {range(alignmentFacts.corErr)}. Its closed-form interval does not: it covers the truth
          only {range(alignmentFacts.covClosed, pct)} of the time, because summaries of one
          article are correlated and the judge's error rates move from article to article.
          Resampling whole documents restores {range(alignmentFacts.covBoot, pct)}. The design
          now requires that interval, and a stratified validation report before a grader can be
          pinned.
        </p>
        <p class="method-note">
          Method and data: <code>scripts/study-eval-alignment/analyze.py</code> downloads the
          inputs from pinned commits, checks their SHA-256, and writes
          <code>src/data/studies/eval-alignment.json</code>. Bootstrap:
          {alignment.meta.bootstrap}. Method after Elangovan et al. (ICLR 2025).
        </p>
